-- title:
--   Infinite adaptive runs with state-dependent thresholds
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals.
--
--   1. **Adaptive metric run.** Let $q\ge1$ targets $t_r$ in a metric space lie in the closure of $\mathrm{eval}(\mathrm{family}(m))$ for every threshold $m$. Suppose every admissible choice has a legal transition. Then there are states and selections, with thresholds read from the current state, such that
--   $$
--   \mathrm{dist}\bigl(\mathrm{eval}(a_i),t_{i\bmod q}\bigr)<\frac1{i+1}
--   $$
--   and each step is a legal transition.
--   2. **Cyclic convergence.** Along the residue class $qn+r$, the evaluations converge to $t_r$.
